---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="SPF Mechanisms Explained: include, a, mx, ptr, exists, ip4, ip6"
  description="SPF mechanisms explained: include, a, mx, ptr, exists, ip4, ip6, all. Qualifiers, syntax and lookup cost. RFC 7208."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">SPF Mechanisms Explained</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">SPF Mechanisms Explained</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-dim">Free SPF mechanism reference: every mechanism in RFC 7208 (include, a, mx, ptr, exists, ip4, ip6, all) explained with syntax and lookup cost.</p>

    <!-- Visual Centerpiece: Reference Table -->
    <section class="not-prose my-8" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class='pdns-table' style='width:100%'><thead><tr><th class='whitespace-nowrap'>Mechanism</th><th class='whitespace-nowrap'>Syntax</th><th class='whitespace-nowrap'>DNS Lookup</th><th class='whitespace-nowrap'>What it matches</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class='pd-mono'>include</td><td>include:domain.com</td><td>Yes</td><td>The SPF record of domain.com (recursive)</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>a</td><td>a[:domain] [/cidr]</td><td>Yes</td><td>A records of domain (or current domain)</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>mx</td><td>mx[:domain] [/cidr]</td><td>Yes</td><td>MX hosts of domain, then their A records</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>ptr</td><td>ptr[:domain]</td><td>Yes (deprecated)</td><td>Reverse DNS lookup of the sender IP</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>ip4</td><td>ip4:1.2.3.0/24</td><td>No</td><td>IPv4 address or range</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>ip6</td><td>ip6:2001:db8::/32</td><td>No</td><td>IPv6 address or range</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>exists</td><td>exists:domain.com</td><td>Yes</td><td>A record exists at the given domain</td></tr><tr><td class='pd-mono'>all</td><td>all</td><td>No</td><td>Matches everything (always last)</td></tr></tbody></table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Factual Content (3-Sentence Rule) -->
    <article class="rich-text mt-10 max-w-none">
      <h2>Technical details</h2>
      <p class='mt-3'>An SPF mechanism is a single evaluation rule inside a v=spf1 TXT record defined in RFC 7208 that determines whether a sending IP is authorized to send email for a domain.</p><p class='mt-3'>SPF mechanisms are evaluated left to right, and the first mechanism that matches the connecting IP address determines the result (pass, fail, softfail, or neutral).</p><p class='mt-3'>The 10-DNS-lookup limit in RFC 7208 caps the total number of mechanisms that trigger DNS queries (include, a, mx, ptr, and exists) to prevent denial-of-service amplification.</p>

      <aside class="mt-8 rounded-xl border-l-3 border-l-violet/50 bg-violet/5 p-5" aria-label="Practitioner field notes">
        <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-violet mb-2">Field notes</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim italic">In my experience auditing client SPF records, the single most common mistake is stacking multiple include: mechanisms for services that were decommissioned years ago. Every stale include burns one of your 10 lookups and increases the risk that a vendor IP rotation pushes you over the limit silently.</p>
      </aside>

      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How many SPF mechanisms can I have?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">You can have unlimited non-DNS mechanisms (ip4, ip6, all). DNS-querying mechanisms (include, a, mx, ptr, exists) are capped at 10 total.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What happens if I use two all mechanisms?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Only the first all mechanism is evaluated. The second is ignored. RFC 7208 requires all to be the last mechanism.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does include: recursive counting apply to nested includes?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes. Each include: triggers a new SPF evaluation of the target domain, and its own DNS-querying mechanisms count against your total of 10.</p></details>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-8 text-xs text-ink-faint">
        Check your domain with our free tools:
        <a href="/spf-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF checker</a>,
        <a href="/dmarc-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC checker</a>,
        <a href="/dkim-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DKIM checker</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
